# Building datasets I: create-dataset and the four shapes of import
prompt: "Why does `import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex` need no date while `import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage` needs two? What is the difference between a fixed variable, a status variable measured on a date, an accumulated variable measured over a period and an event variable? What happens to the rows of your dataset the first time you import — and why is the first import the one that defines the population?"
next: {"title":"Building datasets II: collapse and merge, or how to change the unit of analysis","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/microdata/building-datasets-ii-collapse-and-merge.cast"}

## Building datasets I — 1. The empty dataset
voice: female
level: advanced

    ```microdata md width 960 font_size 17 show code lines 6 frame screen marks ["BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex","2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31",{"text":"import db/INNTEKT_WLONN as wage","kind":"strike"}] draw.mode type hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage
    // refused: import db/INNTEKT_WLONN as wage
    ```

Across these five parts we build the gender-gap dataset from nothing, and see why a variable's shape in time — not your taste — dictates what its import line looks like.
    card title "Building datasets I" subtitle "1. The empty dataset"

We are back in the script from lecture one. Line one is the handshake: version fifty-four of the FDB datastore, addressed from here on as db — and check the catalogue for the current version before you run it.
    draw md md_line_1 parallel true

Then create-dataset persons. This is the line people misread, because it fetches nothing at all.
    draw md_line_2

How many rows does persons hold at this moment — five million, or none?

    pause 1.8

None. Zero rows, zero columns: a named, empty frame on the server, and your only handle on it.
    highlight md_line_2 effect glow

And you will never see those rows — a microdata dataset is something you compute on, not a spreadsheet you scroll.
    point at.ref md_line_2 gesture underline

Now the first real import: sex, from BEFOLKNING_KJOENN — and notice what is missing from the line.
    draw md_line_3

Variable, alias, and nothing else. No date anywhere in it.
    draw md_mark_1

Ask yourself what a date would even mean there — sex on the first of January 2015, as opposed to sex in March?

    pause 1.5

Now wage income for those same people, and suddenly the line carries two dates.
    draw md_line_4

First of January to the thirty-first of December: wage income is not a state you can photograph, it is a sum accumulated across that window.
    draw md_mark_2

    quiz "Why does INNTEKT_WLONN take a period while BEFOLKNING_KJOENN takes no date?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Wage income exists only as a sum over an interval, so the interval is part of the value; sex has one value and no time index at all.
        wrong: It is not about update frequency or speed — it is about whether the variable has a value at an instant, over a span, or outside time.
        * Income data are updated more often
        + Income is accumulated over an interval; sex is fixed in time
        * Two dates make the query run faster
        * The datastore holds income only yearly

Which is why this version, written without a period, is not merely sloppy.
    draw md_line_5

There is no such number as a person's wage income at an instant, so the script is refused rather than quietly handed a default year.
    draw md_mark_3

And municipality sits between the two: one value at a time, so exactly one date — one shape per line, and there turn out to be four of them.

    quiz "What does create-dataset persons put into your dataset?"
        right: It creates an empty named dataset; rows appear only with the first import — which is what makes that first import so consequential.
        * Everyone in the population
        + Nothing — it stays empty until the first import
        * The variables named later in the script

So before you type import, answer one question: does this variable have a value at an instant, over a span, or no time at all? Part two names all four shapes.
    highlight md_line_3 md_line_4 effect pulse delivery grave

## Four shapes of a variable
voice: female
level: advanced

    text title "Four shapes of a variable" x 500 y 712 font_size 32 hidden true
    arrow axis from.x 230 from.y 120 to.x 880 to.y 120 hidden true
    text time_cap "calendar time" #8f887c x 800 y 62 font_size 20 hidden true
    text tlab_a "2015-01-01" #8f887c x 390 y 88 font_size 19 hidden true
    text tlab_b "2015-07-01" #8f887c x 550 y 88 font_size 19 hidden true
    text tlab_c "2015-12-31" #8f887c x 708 y 88 font_size 19 hidden true
    path guide_a dashed #8f887c points [[390,128],[390,470]] hidden true
    path guide_b dashed #8f887c points [[550,128],[550,470]] hidden true
    path guide_c dashed #8f887c points [[708,128],[708,380]] hidden true
    text name_fixed "FIXED" #8a5fa8 x 118 y 560 font_size 24 hidden true
    path fixed_line #8a5fa8 points [[205,560],[878,560]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label cap_fixed "sex — BEFOLKNING_KJOENN" above #8a5fa8 attach_to fixed_line hidden true
    text n_fixed "no date" #8a5fa8 x 932 y 560 font_size 19 hidden true
    text name_status "STATUS" #2f6b8f x 118 y 455 font_size 24 hidden true
    path status_line dashed #8f887c points [[230,455],[870,455]] style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    shape status_dot #2f6b8f shape circle x 390 y 455 radius 11 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    shape status_dot_2 #2f6b8f shape circle x 550 y 455 radius 11 style.fill #2f6b8f hidden true
    label cap_status "kommune — BEFOLKNING_KOMMNR_FORMELL" above #2f6b8f attach_to status_dot hidden true
    label val_status_1 "0301 Oslo" below #2f6b8f attach_to status_dot hidden true
    label val_status_2 "4601 Bergen" below #2f6b8f attach_to status_dot_2 hidden true
    text n_status "one date" #2f6b8f x 932 y 455 font_size 19 hidden true
    text name_accum "ACCUMULATED" #b5482e x 118 y 352 font_size 20 hidden true
    path accum_line dashed #8f887c points [[230,352],[870,352]] style.stroke_width 1.5 hidden true
    shape accum_band #b5482e shape rect x 549 y 352 width 318 height 46 style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    text val_accum "486 000 kr" x 549 y 352 font_size 22 hidden true
    label cap_accum "wage — INNTEKT_WLONN" above #b5482e attach_to accum_band hidden true

Now that we have an empty dataset waiting for its first variable, let's ask what a datastore variable actually is — because every one of them has a shape in time.
    draw title

    draw axis tlab_a tlab_b tlab_c time_cap

Our example throughout: one woman from the 1985 cohort. Four facts about her — and four different shapes. Start with her sex, BEFOLKNING_KJOENN. On which date would you measure it?

    pause 1.8

There is no date to give: the datastore holds one value per person, with no time dimension at all, so the line runs straight past both edges of the calendar.
    draw name_fixed fixed_line cap_fixed

And fixed is a storage decision, not a claim about biology — the variable simply has nowhere to put a change.
    draw n_fixed

    pause 0.4

Next, her municipality. She lived in Oslo in January 2015 and in Bergen by July. So what is her municipality?

    pause 1.8

The question was incomplete. A status variable is a state read at an instant — on the first of January she is 0301, Oslo.
    draw name_status status_line status_dot guide_a val_status_1 cap_status

Read the very same variable on the first of July and she is 4601, Bergen. One date in, one value out.
    draw status_dot_2 guide_b val_status_2 n_status

    pause 0.4

Her wage income for 2015 was 486 thousand kroner. So what was her wage income on the twelfth of March?

    pause 1.8

That question has no answer. Wage income is not a state but a flow, accumulated over an interval — the number belongs to the whole window you asked for.
    draw name_accum accum_line accum_band guide_c val_accum cap_accum

Ask for only the first half of 2015 and the same person and the same variable give a smaller number — which is why an accumulated variable needs a start and an end.
    text n_accum "two dates" #b5482e x 932 y 352 font_size 19

If you come from national accounting, you already own this distinction: a status variable is a stock, an accumulated variable is a flow. The register writes it into the import itself.
    highlight status_dot accum_band effect glow

    quiz "You import INNTEKT_WLONN for a single instant, 2015-06-30. What can the datastore return?" intro "A quick check."
        right: A flow only has a value over an interval, so an instant marks no span for anything to accumulate in.
        wrong: An accumulated variable is a flow, not a state — a single instant defines no amount.
        * Her wage that day
        + Nothing meaningful — a flow needs an interval
        * The year's total divided by 365

And some variables are neither a state nor a total. Her sick-leave spell began on the ninth of March and ended in May. Which date do you ask for?

    pause 1.7

Neither: an event variable arrives as records with their own start and end dates. You give a window, but the timing inside it is data, not something you choose.
    text name_event "EVENT" #87a878 x 118 y 235 font_size 24
    path event_line dashed #8f887c points [[230,235],[870,235]] style.stroke_width 1.5
    shape event_1 #87a878 shape circle x 300 y 235 radius 10 style.fill #87a878
    shape event_spell #87a878 shape rect x 476 y 235 width 67 height 16 style.fill #87a878
    shape event_3 #87a878 shape circle x 790 y 235 radius 10 style.fill #87a878
    label cap_event "each record carries its own dates" above #87a878 attach_to event_line
    text n_event "its own dates" #87a878 x 932 y 235 font_size 19

    erase guide_a guide_b guide_c

These two middle rows are the ones people quietly conflate: a point on a line, and a stretch of line — a stock you read, and a flow you sum.
    focus name_status status_line status_dot status_dot_2 name_accum accum_line accum_band val_accum

The shape decides how much time information the measurement carries: none, one instant, one interval, or the data's own clock.
    highlight n_fixed n_status n_accum n_event effect glow

    quiz "NUDB_BU, highest completed education — which shape is it?"
        right: Education is a state that can change, so it is read on a date — her degree in 2015 need not be her degree in 2025.
        wrong: People keep studying: the level is a state with a date, not a lifetime constant.
        * Fixed
        + Status on a date
        * Accumulated over a period

So before writing a single line you can now ask of any variable in the catalogue: does this measurement live outside time, at an instant, over an interval — or does it bring its own dates? In the next part, that reading becomes the syntax.
    highlight fixed_line status_dot accum_band event_spell effect pulse

## Shape dictates syntax
voice: female
level: advanced

    text title_t "Shape dictates syntax" x 500 y 722 font_size 30 hidden true
    ```microdata md x 500 y 355 width 950 font_size 15 show below lines 8 frame screen marks ["import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex","2015-01-01 as kommune","2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31"] hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as birth
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KOMMNR_FORMELL 2015-01-01 as kommune
    import db/NUDB_BU 2015-01-01 as educ
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage
    summarize wage
    ```

Now that we have seen the four shapes a variable can take, watch what each shape does to the line you type — get the dates wrong and the script still runs, quietly answering a different question.
    draw title_t md md_line_1 parallel true

One empty dataset called persons — every import from here lands in it.
    draw md_line_2

Sex, and year of birth. Before I type them: how many dates do you think those two lines need?

    pause 1.8

None. A fixed variable holds one value for the whole of a person's register life, so there is no moment to name.
    draw md_line_3 md_line_4

The whole line is variable, alias, and nothing else — the syntax has no slot where a date could go.
    draw md_mark_1

Municipality next. Our cohort born in 1985 turns thirty in 2015, and people that age move. What does that do to the line?

    pause 1.8

One date. A status variable has a value at every instant, so the import only means something once you name the instant.
    draw md_line_5

On the first of January 2015 this person is registered in 0301, Oslo. Had they moved to Stavanger in June, the same variable read in December would return 1103.
    draw md_mark_2

Highest completed education takes the same signature — the degree as it stood on that date, not the one finished three years later.
    draw md_line_6

And wage income for 2015. What date would you put on that one?

    pause 1.8

Two dates — a span. Wage income is accumulated: not a state you are in, but a sum that builds between two instants.
    draw md_line_7

And that span is not a filter on rows; it is part of the number's definition — kroner earned between those two dates.
    draw md_mark_3

Summarize proves the shape: one wage figure per person for the whole year, never twelve monthly ones.
    draw md_line_8 md_out

    quiz "Which signature does INNTEKT_WLONN require?" intro "A quick check."
        right: A span — wage income is accumulated, so the two dates define the number itself.
        wrong: Wage income is not a state held at an instant; it is a sum over a window.
        * No date
        * One date
        + A from–to span

But two of the four shapes share one signature, and that is where scripts go quietly wrong.
    clear keep ["title_t"]

Here is the accumulated span again: everything inside the window is summed into a single number.
    text sig_a "import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage" x 500 y 580 font_size 21
    text note_a "accumulated: the window is SUMMED into one number" below sig_a gap 26 #2f6b8f font_size 22

And here is an event variable — the same two dates, a completely different meaning: the window selects which events come back, with their dates of occurrence.
    text sig_b "import db/<EVENT_VARIABLE> 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as move" below note_a gap 54 font_size 21
    text note_b "event: the window SELECTS which events come back" below sig_b gap 26 #b5482e font_size 22

Nothing in the syntax separates them; only the variable's shape in the metadata catalogue does — which is why lecture two sent you there first.
    highlight sig_a sig_b effect glow

And these shapes are not the language's invention — the population register logs changes as they happen, so you may ask it for any date you like.
    box pop_reg "Population register" x 275 y 250 width 330 height 80
    text pop_conseq "logs changes as they happen → any date" below pop_reg gap 22 #8f887c font_size 20

The tax register closes an annual assessment, so wage income exists only as a whole year. There is no Tuesday to ask about.
    box tax_reg "Tax register" x 725 y 250 width 330 height 80
    text tax_conseq "closes once a year → one span" below tax_reg gap 22 #8f887c font_size 20

    quiz "Why does wage income refuse a single date?"
        right: Income exists in the register only as an annual accumulation, so a span is the only honest way to ask for it.
        wrong: It is not a rule of the language — it is how the tax register keeps its books.
        * The language reserves single dates for population variables
        + The tax register only closes a full-year assessment
        * Monthly wages are too confidential to release

So the number of dates in an import is never style and never a filter — it is the shape of someone else's bookkeeping showing through your syntax.
    text keeper "The date is not a filter. It is part of what the number is." x 500 y 110 font_size 26
    mark keeper_box target keeper kind box
    delivery grave

## The first import makes the population
voice: female
level: advanced

    ```microdata md x 260 y 420 width 500 font_size 14 show code lines 6 frame screen marks ["import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage"] hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as birth
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage
    ```
    shape tbl_box shape rect x 755 y 495 width 460 height 270 hidden true
    label tbl_title "dataset: persons" above attach_to tbl_box hidden true
    path rule #8f887c points [[530,578],[980,578]] hidden true
    text h_person "person" #8f887c x 583 y 600 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_birth "birth" #8f887c x 698 y 600 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_sex "sex" #8f887c x 813 y 600 font_size 22 hidden true
    text h_wage "wage" #8f887c x 928 y 600 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r1_id "#1" x 583 y 538 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r2_id "#2" x 583 y 490 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r3_id "#3" x 583 y 442 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r4_id "#4" x 583 y 394 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r1_birth "1985-03" x 698 y 538 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r2_birth "1985-07" x 698 y 490 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r3_birth "1985-11" x 698 y 442 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r4_birth "2019-05" x 698 y 394 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r1_sex "1" x 813 y 538 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r2_sex "2" x 813 y 490 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r3_sex "2" x 813 y 442 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r4_sex "1" x 813 y 394 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r1_wage "512 000" x 928 y 538 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r2_wage "388 000" x 928 y 490 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r3_wage "." #8f887c x 928 y 442 font_size 22 hidden true
    text r4_wage "." #8f887c x 928 y 394 font_size 22 hidden true
    group col_id members ["h_person","r1_id","r2_id","r3_id","r4_id"] hidden true
    group col_birth members ["h_birth","r1_birth","r2_birth","r3_birth","r4_birth"] hidden true
    group col_sex members ["h_sex","r1_sex","r2_sex","r3_sex","r4_sex"] hidden true
    group col_wage members ["h_wage","r1_wage","r2_wage","r3_wage","r4_wage"] hidden true
    shape pop_all #3d3833 shape rect x 640 y 440 width 180 height 280 style.fill #87a878 hidden true
    shape pop_wage #b5482e shape rect x 880 y 381 width 180 height 162 style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    label lab_all "All persons" above attach_to pop_all hidden true
    label lab_wage "Wage earners 2015" above attach_to pop_wage hidden true
    text note "a gap among earners only" #b5482e x 755 y 240 font_size 24 hidden true

Now that we have seen how each shape dictates its syntax, let's follow the rows — because the very first import quietly decides who is in your study.
    draw md md_line_1 md_line_2 parallel true

create-dataset persons gives you this: a table with no columns and, the part people forget, no rows at all.
    draw tbl_box tbl_title

So when the first import runs, where do the rows actually come from?
    point at.ref tbl_box gesture circle

    pause 1.6

From the variable itself: every unit in the register that has a value for birth year and month becomes one row — one person, several million of them.
    draw md_line_3 col_id

The values arrive with the rows, and notice the fourth person, born in twenty nineteen: a fixed variable carries no time limit, so the population is everyone the register has ever held.
    draw col_birth

You never see these identifiers — microdata returns tables, never rows — so your population is something you reason about, not something you can look at.
    highlight col_id effect glow

The next line imports sex, which also covers everyone in the register. Does the table get taller, or only wider?
    highlight tbl_box effect pulse

    pause 1.6

Only wider: every import after the first is a join onto rows that already exist — raw codes here, one and two, labels come later in the course.
    draw md_line_4 col_sex

Now wage income for twenty fifteen, an accumulated variable measured over a whole period — and two of our four cells come back empty.
    draw md_line_5 col_wage

A woman born in eighty-five with no wage that year, and a child born four years after the period: the rows survive, the cells are system missing — which you test with sysmiss of wage, never with wage equals dot.
    highlight r3_wage r4_wage effect circle

    quiz "A person has wage income in 2015 but no row in persons. What does the wage import do with them?"
        intro: A quick check before we go on.
        right: Nothing — a later import only fills cells on rows that already exist, so that person stays invisible for the rest of the script.
        wrong: No import after the first can create a row; it can only fill cells on rows that are already there.
        * Adds a new row for them
        * Attaches the value to the nearest row
        + Nothing at all

So — what if this line had been the first import instead?
    draw md_mark_1

    pause 1.6

Then the population would be defined as people with a wage record in twenty fifteen, and these two would never have existed.
    erase r3_id r3_birth r3_sex r3_wage r4_id r4_birth r4_sex r4_wage parallel true

And nothing later brings them back: no filter, no further import, puts a row into a table that never had one.
    highlight col_wage effect glow

    clear keep ["md","md_line_1","md_line_2","md_line_3","md_line_4","md_line_5","md_mark_1"]

The two populations are nowhere near the same size: the register holds more than five million people, while wage income in twenty fifteen exists for roughly three million — so the gender gap you go on to measure would be a gap among earners only.
    draw pop_all lab_all pop_wage lab_wage note delivery grave

    quiz "Why does that earners-only population make the measured gender wage gap look smaller?"
        right: Zero-earning women drop out of the table entirely, so the gap is computed only among those who do earn.
        wrong: Think about who is missing: the people with no wage record at all, and who those people mostly are.
        * Wages are top-coded
        + Women with no wage income are not in the data
        * Men are more often missing

create-dataset makes the shell, the first import makes the people, and every line after it can only fill cells — so choosing your first import is choosing your population.
    highlight md_line_3 effect glow

## The 1985 cohort, assembled
voice: female
level: advanced

    ```microdata md width 940 show left lines 8 frame screen marks ["import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as born","2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31"] draw.mode type hidden true
    // The 1985 cohort - one row per person
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_FOEDSELS_AAR_MND as born
    destring born
    keep if born >= 198501
    keep if born <= 198512
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KOMMNR_FORMELL 2015-01-01 as kommune
    import db/NUDB_BU 2015-10-01 as educ
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2015-01-01 to 2015-12-31 as wage
    // event variables would add rows, not columns
    summarize wage
    ```

Now that we have seen the first import write the rows, here is the whole lecture as one script — and the order of its lines silently decides who your gender gap is even about.
    draw md md_line_1 md_line_2 parallel true

create-dataset gives us persons: a named frame, columns still to come, and so far not a single row.
    draw md_line_3

Sex and birth month are both fixed traits with no date. Which of them has to be imported first?

    pause 1.8

Birth month — because this import decides who exists, and everyone in the population register carries it.
    draw md_line_4

destring makes it numeric, and the two keeps cut the rows to the 1985 cohort — a little over sixty thousand people.
    draw md_line_5 md_line_6 md_line_7

Every later line is now a column hung on those rows, never a new set of people.
    draw md_mark_1

So sex simply attaches: a fixed trait, no date, because registered sex is not a fact about a particular Tuesday.
    draw md_line_8

Municipality only means something relative to a moment, so we ask where each person was registered on the first of January 2015.
    draw md_line_9

Education is status too, but the education register is stamped in October — that is the date it can honestly answer for.
    draw md_line_10

Then why can wage income not be asked for on a single date at all?

    pause 1.6

Because nobody has a wage on a date — they have wage earned between two dates, so an accumulated variable takes a from and a to.
    draw md_line_11

That window is the measurement: narrow it to one quarter and the same person returns a different number, with nothing else in the script changed.
    draw md_mark_2

    quiz "Why does INNTEKT_WLONN need two dates when BEFOLKNING_KOMMNR_FORMELL needs one?"
        right: Wage income is a sum over an interval, so the interval has to be stated; municipality is a status you can read off on a single day.
        wrong: It is not about update frequency — it is about what kind of quantity the variable is.
        + It is accumulated over a period
        * It changes too often for one date
        * The income register updates twice a year

The fourth shape would break this table outright: an event — a move, a birth, a spell of unemployment — arrives as one row per occurrence, which is why it waits until lecture nine.
    draw md_line_12

And summarize confirms the frame survived intact: one row per person, one 2015 wage each.
    draw md_line_13 md_out

    pause 0.5

    clear

Strip the script away and what remains is a checklist to run before writing any import at all.
    text head "Before you write an import" x 500 y 645 font_size 34

Fixed traits take no date; status variables take exactly one, and it must be a date the register can defend.
    text c1 "Fixed trait  →  no date" below head gap 42 #2f6b8f font_size 28
    text c2 "Status on a date  →  one date" below c1 gap 26 #8a5fa8 font_size 28

Accumulated variables take two, because the period is the measurement; event variables take a window and hand you rows instead of columns.
    text c3 "Accumulated over a period  →  two dates" below c2 gap 26 #b5482e font_size 28
    text c4 "Event  →  a window, and extra rows" below c3 gap 26 #d0865f font_size 28

And above all four: the first import writes the rows.
    text c5 "The first import writes the rows" below c4 gap 48 font_size 30
    mark ann target c5 kind box

    quiz "You open an empty dataset and import NUDB_BU first. What population have you defined?"
        right: Your population becomes everyone with a value in that register — people with no registered education never enter, and nothing later in the script will tell you they are missing.
        wrong: The dataset starts with no rows, so the rows come from whoever the first imported variable covers.
        * Everyone in Norway
        + Everyone with a registered education
        * The 1985 cohort

So a script is never a list of variables you wanted: it is a population chosen by your first import, measured on dates you chose on purpose — and next lecture we change the unit of analysis underneath it.
    highlight c5 effect glow delivery grave

