    link end_again href lecture:3 title "Watch again" form text open here x 500 y 160
    text end_count "3 of 10" x 500 y 95 font_size 22 style.opacity 0.6
---
audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    '|a||Building datasets I: create-dataset and the four shapes of import':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    female|a||Across these five parts we build the gender-gap dataset from nothing, and see why a variable's shape in time — not your taste — dictates what its import line looks like.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||We are back in the script from lecture one. Line one is the handshake: version fifty-four of the FDB datastore, addressed from here on as db — and check the catalogue for the current version before you run it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Then create-dataset persons. This is the line people misread, because it fetches nothing at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||How many rows does persons hold at this moment — five million, or none?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||None. Zero rows, zero columns: a named, empty frame on the server, and your only handle on it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And you will never see those rows — a microdata dataset is something you compute on, not a spreadsheet you scroll.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Now the first real import: sex, from BEFOLKNING_KJOENN — and notice what is missing from the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Variable, alias, and nothing else. No date anywhere in it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Ask yourself what a date would even mean there — sex on the first of January 2015, as opposed to sex in March?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now wage income for those same people, and suddenly the line carries two dates.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||First of January to the thirty-first of December: wage income is not a state you can photograph, it is a sum accumulated across that window.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A quick check. Why does INNTEKT_WLONN take a period while BEFOLKNING_KJOENN takes no date?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Wage income exists only as a sum over an interval, so the interval is part of the value; sex has one value and no time index at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Which is why this version, written without a period, is not merely sloppy.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||There is no such number as a person's wage income at an instant, so the script is refused rather than quietly handed a default year.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And municipality sits between the two: one value at a time, so exactly one date — one shape per line, and there turn out to be four of them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||What does create-dataset persons put into your dataset?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||It creates an empty named dataset; rows appear only with the first import — which is what makes that first import so consequential.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|So before you type import, answer one question: does this variable have a value at an instant, over a span, or no time at all? Part two names all four shapes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now that we have an empty dataset waiting for its first variable, let's ask what a datastore variable actually is — because every one of them has a shape in time.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Our example throughout: one woman from the 1985 cohort. Four facts about her — and four different shapes. Start with her sex, BEFOLKNING_KJOENN. On which date would you measure it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||There is no date to give: the datastore holds one value per person, with no time dimension at all, so the line runs straight past both edges of the calendar.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And fixed is a storage decision, not a claim about biology — the variable simply has nowhere to put a change.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Next, her municipality. She lived in Oslo in January 2015 and in Bergen by July. So what is her municipality?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The question was incomplete. A status variable is a state read at an instant — on the first of January she is 0301, Oslo.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Read the very same variable on the first of July and she is 4601, Bergen. One date in, one value out.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Her wage income for 2015 was 486 thousand kroner. So what was her wage income on the twelfth of March?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||That question has no answer. Wage income is not a state but a flow, accumulated over an interval — the number belongs to the whole window you asked for.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Ask for only the first half of 2015 and the same person and the same variable give a smaller number — which is why an accumulated variable needs a start and an end.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||If you come from national accounting, you already own this distinction: a status variable is a stock, an accumulated variable is a flow. The register writes it into the import itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A quick check. You import INNTEKT_WLONN for a single instant, 2015-06-30. What can the datastore return?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A flow only has a value over an interval, so an instant marks no span for anything to accumulate in.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And some variables are neither a state nor a total. Her sick-leave spell began on the ninth of March and ended in May. Which date do you ask for?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Neither: an event variable arrives as records with their own start and end dates. You give a window, but the timing inside it is data, not something you choose.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||These two middle rows are the ones people quietly conflate: a point on a line, and a stretch of line — a stock you read, and a flow you sum.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The shape decides how much time information the measurement carries: none, one instant, one interval, or the data''s own clock.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||NUDB_BU, highest completed education — which shape is it?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Education is a state that can change, so it is read on a date — her degree in 2015 need not be her degree in 2025.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So before writing a single line you can now ask of any variable in the catalogue: does this measurement live outside time, at an instant, over an interval — or does it bring its own dates? In the next part, that reading becomes the syntax.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now that we have seen the four shapes a variable can take, watch what each shape does to the line you type — get the dates wrong and the script still runs, quietly answering a different question.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One empty dataset called persons — every import from here lands in it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Sex, and year of birth. Before I type them: how many dates do you think those two lines need?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||None. A fixed variable holds one value for the whole of a person's register life, so there is no moment to name.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The whole line is variable, alias, and nothing else — the syntax has no slot where a date could go.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Municipality next. Our cohort born in 1985 turns thirty in 2015, and people that age move. What does that do to the line?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||One date. A status variable has a value at every instant, so the import only means something once you name the instant.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||On the first of January 2015 this person is registered in 0301, Oslo. Had they moved to Stavanger in June, the same variable read in December would return 1103.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Highest completed education takes the same signature — the degree as it stood on that date, not the one finished three years later.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And wage income for 2015. What date would you put on that one?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Two dates — a span. Wage income is accumulated: not a state you are in, but a sum that builds between two instants.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And that span is not a filter on rows; it is part of the number's definition — kroner earned between those two dates.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Summarize proves the shape: one wage figure per person for the whole year, never twelve monthly ones.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A quick check. Which signature does INNTEKT_WLONN require?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A span — wage income is accumulated, so the two dates define the number itself.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||But two of the four shapes share one signature, and that is where scripts go quietly wrong.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Here is the accumulated span again: everything inside the window is summed into a single number.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And here is an event variable — the same two dates, a completely different meaning: the window selects which events come back, with their dates of occurrence.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Nothing in the syntax separates them; only the variable's shape in the metadata catalogue does — which is why lecture two sent you there first.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||And these shapes are not the language's invention — the population register logs changes as they happen, so you may ask it for any date you like.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The tax register closes an annual assessment, so wage income exists only as a whole year. There is no Tuesday to ask about.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Why does wage income refuse a single date?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Income exists in the register only as an annual accumulation, so a span is the only honest way to ask for it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a|grave|So the number of dates in an import is never style and never a filter — it is the shape of someone else's bookkeeping showing through your syntax.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now that we have seen how each shape dictates its syntax, let's follow the rows — because the very first import quietly decides who is in your study.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||create-dataset persons gives you this: a table with no columns and, the part people forget, no rows at all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||So when the first import runs, where do the rows actually come from?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||From the variable itself: every unit in the register that has a value for birth year and month becomes one row — one person, several million of them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The values arrive with the rows, and notice the fourth person, born in twenty nineteen: a fixed variable carries no time limit, so the population is everyone the register has ever held.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||You never see these identifiers — microdata returns tables, never rows — so your population is something you reason about, not something you can look at.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||The next line imports sex, which also covers everyone in the register. Does the table get taller, or only wider?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||Only wider: every import after the first is a join onto rows that already exist — raw codes here, one and two, labels come later in the course.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now wage income for twenty fifteen, an accumulated variable measured over a whole period — and two of our four cells come back empty.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||A woman born in eighty-five with no wage that year, and a child born four years after the period: the rows survive, the cells are system missing — which you test with sysmiss of wage, never with wage equals dot.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||A quick check before we go on. A person has wage income in 2015 but no row in persons. What does the wage import do with them?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Nothing — a later import only fills cells on rows that already exist, so that person stays invisible for the rest of the script.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||So — what if this line had been the first import instead?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Then the population would be defined as people with a wage record in twenty fifteen, and these two would never have existed.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And nothing later brings them back: no filter, no further import, puts a row into a table that never had one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|The two populations are nowhere near the same size: the register holds more than five million people, while wage income in twenty fifteen exists for roughly three million — so the gender gap you go on to measure would be a gap among earners only.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Why does that earners-only population make the measured gender wage gap look smaller?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Zero-earning women drop out of the table entirely, so the gap is computed only among those who do earn.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||create-dataset makes the shell, the first import makes the people, and every line after it can only fill cells — so choosing your first import is choosing your population.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Now that we have seen the first import write the rows, here is the whole lecture as one script — and the order of its lines silently decides who your gender gap is even about.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||create-dataset gives us persons: a named frame, columns still to come, and so far not a single row.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Sex and birth month are both fixed traits with no date. Which of them has to be imported first?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Birth month — because this import decides who exists, and everyone in the population register carries it.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||destring makes it numeric, and the two keeps cut the rows to the 1985 cohort — a little over sixty thousand people.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Every later line is now a column hung on those rows, never a new set of people.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||So sex simply attaches: a fixed trait, no date, because registered sex is not a fact about a particular Tuesday.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Municipality only means something relative to a moment, so we ask where each person was registered on the first of January 2015.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Education is status too, but the education register is stamped in October — that is the date it can honestly answer for.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Then why can wage income not be asked for on a single date at all?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Because nobody has a wage on a date — they have wage earned between two dates, so an accumulated variable takes a from and a to.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||That window is the measurement: narrow it to one quarter and the same person returns a different number, with nothing else in the script changed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Why does INNTEKT_WLONN need two dates when BEFOLKNING_KOMMNR_FORMELL needs one?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Wage income is a sum over an interval, so the interval has to be stated; municipality is a status you can read off on a single day.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||The fourth shape would break this table outright: an event — a move, a birth, a spell of unemployment — arrives as one row per occurrence, which is why it waits until lecture nine.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And summarize confirms the frame survived intact: one row per person, one 2015 wage each.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Strip the script away and what remains is a checklist to run before writing any import at all.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Fixed traits take no date; status variables take exactly one, and it must be a date the register can defend.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Accumulated variables take two, because the period is the measurement; event variables take a window and hand you rows instead of columns.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a||And above all four: the first import writes the rows.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||You open an empty dataset and import NUDB_BU first. What population have you defined?:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    female|a||Your population becomes everyone with a value in that register — people with no registered education never enter, and nothing later in the script will tell you they are missing.:
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    'female|a|grave|So a script is never a list of variables you wanted: it is a population chosen by your first import, measured on dates you chose on purpose — and next lecture we change the unit of analysis underneath it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Next: Building datasets II: collapse and merge, or how to change the unit of analysis':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
